WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:06.094
My plunge into the sea was so unexpected that I can't remember what I was thinking at the time.

2
00:00:06.095 --> 00:00:09.213
I'm a good swimmer, so I know I didn't panic.

3
00:00:09.214 --> 00:00:13.626
Once I was back at the surface, my first thought was to look for my ship.

4
00:00:13.627 --> 00:00:20.443
Had the crew seen me fall? Had the Abraham Lincoln turned around? Did I have any hope of being saved?

5
00:00:20.444 --> 00:00:29.030
I caught a glimpse of a ship disappearing in the east, its lights dying out in the distance. It was the Abraham Lincoln ! I was

6
00:00:29.031 --> 00:00:32.102
doomed. "Help! Help!" I

7
00:00:32.103 --> 00:00:39.767
shouted. I swam desperately toward the ship, but my clothes made it difficult to move. I was sinking . . . I was

8
00:00:39.768 --> 00:00:41.348
suffocating!

9
00:00:41.349 --> 00:00:45.499
"Help!" That was my last cry before I started to slip below the

10
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:51.761
waves. Suddenly a strong hand seized my clothes, and I felt myself being pulled to the

11
00:00:51.762 --> 00:00:54.895
surface. Then I heard these words in my ear:

12
00:00:54.896 --> 00:01:00.078
"If you would kindly lean on my shoulder, you'll be able to swim more easily."

13
00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:06.745
"Conseil!" I cried as I seized my faithful servant's arm. "Is that really you?"

14
00:01:06.746 --> 00:01:11.078
"Yes, I'm here awaiting your orders," replied Conseil.

15
00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:14.229
"That horrible collision threw you into the sea too?"

16
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:18.395
"No, but being your servant, I naturally followed you into the water."

17
00:01:18.396 --> 00:01:21.316
"And what about the ship?" I asked.

18
00:01:21.317 --> 00:01:26.911
Conseil flipped over to float on his back. "I don't think you should count on that ship to rescue you.

19
00:01:26.912 --> 00:01:33.070
When I threw myself into the water, I heard the man at the wheel say that both the rudder and propeller were broken."

20
00:01:33.071 --> 00:01:34.387
"Broken?"

21
00:01:34.388 --> 00:01:37.005
"Yes, broken by the monster's teeth.

22
00:01:37.006 --> 00:01:43.792
That's the only damage to the Abraham Lincoln , but it's bad news for us because they can't steer the ship

23
00:01:43.793 --> 00:01:46.387
anymore." "Then we are lost!" I

24
00:01:46.388 --> 00:01:49.706
cried. "Perhaps," Conseil replied

25
00:01:49.706 --> 00:01:56.038
calmly. "But we can survive for at least several hours more, and one can do a lot in several

26
00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:01.451
hours." Conseil's unruffled calmness encouraged me, and I swam more

27
00:02:01.452 --> 00:02:09.697
vigorously. Since the Abraham Lincoln couldn't turn back to rescue us, our only hope was that Captain Farragut had sent out the small boats to search for

28
00:02:09.698 --> 00:02:12.943
us. We vowed to try to wait for as long as

29
00:02:12.944 --> 00:02:19.022
possible. I decided that we should conserve our strength so that the two of us wouldn't be exhausted at the same

30
00:02:19.023 --> 00:02:27.593
time. Here's how we managed this: While one man floated on his back, the other man swam and pushed the floating man ahead of

31
00:02:27.594 --> 00:02:33.419
him. Taking turns for ten minutes at a time, we could swim for hours, perhaps until

32
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:38.268
daybreak. The Abraham Lincoln 's collision with the creature had taken place around eleven o'clock

33
00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:45.165
at night. I reckoned that we had eight hours to swim before sunrise, which we could do if we kept relieving each

34
00:02:45.166 --> 00:02:48.498
other. The sea was very calm, so that was in our

35
00:02:48.499 --> 00:02:53.070
favor. But around one o'clock in the morning, I was overcome with terrible

36
00:02:53.071 --> 00:03:01.324
fatigue. My arms and legs were seized with horrible cramps. Conseil had to keep me afloat and our survival depended on him

37
00:03:01.325 --> 00:03:06.983
alone. I heard the poor fellow panting and I felt that he couldn't keep up this effort much

38
00:03:06.984 --> 00:03:09.475
longer. "Leave me!" I said to

39
00:03:09.476 --> 00:03:14.308
him. "Leave my master? Never," he replied. "I would rather

40
00:03:14.309 --> 00:03:22.380
drown." Just then the moon appeared at the edge of a thick cloud. The surface of the sea glittered with its rays, and we felt

41
00:03:22.381 --> 00:03:26.030
revived. I scanned the horizon and spotted the Abraham

42
00:03:26.031 --> 00:03:31.871
Lincoln ! But there were no small boats on the water, and the ship appeared to be about five

43
00:03:31.872 --> 00:03:39.871
miles away. My swollen lips could make no sound. Conseil could still speak, and periodically I heard him cry out

44
00:03:39.872 --> 00:03:42.617
for help. We stopped moving for a moment

45
00:03:42.618 --> 00:03:47.189
and listened. "Did you hear that?" I murmured. "It sounded like someone

46
00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:52.681
answered you." "Yes! Yes!" said Conseil, and he gave one more

47
00:03:52.682 --> 00:03:57.872
despairing call. This time there was no mistake; a human voice had responded to

48
00:03:57.873 --> 00:04:05.197
Conseil's call. Was it another unfortunate victim who had fallen from the ship? Or was it a small boat hailing us in

49
00:04:05.198 --> 00:04:11.315
the darkness? Leaning on my shoulder, Conseil raised himself half out of the water and then fell

50
00:04:11.316 --> 00:04:13.689
back exhausted. "What did you see?"

51
00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:20.768
I asked. "I saw . . . " he whispered. "I saw . . . But we shouldn't talk. We should save

52
00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:24.268
our strength." Conseil was once more propelling me through

53
00:04:24.269 --> 00:04:32.831
the water. Sometimes he raised his head and uttered a cry that was answered by a voice that came nearer and nearer. I barely heard

54
00:04:32.832 --> 00:04:37.585
it. My strength was exhausted; my mouth opened and filled with salt

55
00:04:37.586 --> 00:04:42.070
water. I raised my head for the last time, and then I sank below the

56
00:04:42.071 --> 00:04:49.078
surface. At this moment I struck a hard body. I clung to it, and then felt that I was being pulled

57
00:04:49.079 --> 00:04:54.237
up. I fainted. But I quickly came to because someone was trying to revive

58
00:04:54.238 --> 00:04:57.888
me. "Conseil," I murmured as I half opened my

59
00:04:57.889 --> 00:05:01.134
eyes. "Did you call me, Master?" asked

60
00:05:01.135 --> 00:05:06.800
Conseil. But it was not Conseil who was vigorously rubbing my arms and legs. It was Ned

61
00:05:06.801 --> 00:05:12.030
Land. "Ned!" I cried. "Were you thrown into the sea by the shock of the

62
00:05:12.031 --> 00:05:21.705
collision?" "Yes, but fortunately I was able to quickly climb onto this island, or, perhaps I should say, climb onto our narwhal," replied the

63
00:05:21.706 --> 00:05:26.363
harpooner. "I soon discovered why my harpoon couldn't penetrate its

64
00:05:26.364 --> 00:05:30.617
skin." "Why, Ned?" I said, thoroughly puzzled by this

65
00:05:30.618 --> 00:05:35.371
conversation. "Because, Professor, the beast is made of sheet

66
00:05:35.372 --> 00:05:40.856
metal." I sat up. The three of us were resting on top of something that was smooth and

67
00:05:40.857 --> 00:05:44.355
black. Most of it was submerged below the surface of the

68
00:05:44.356 --> 00:05:51.268
water. I kicked my heels against our creature and heard a metallic sound in return. There was no doubt about

69
00:05:51.269 --> 00:05:57.998
it. This monster, this creature, this animal that had puzzled the world was

70
00:05:57.999 --> 00:06:04.498
man-made. Ned was convinced that we were lying on the back of a submarine, and Conseil and I could only agree with

71
00:06:04.499 --> 00:06:12.507
him. Just then a bubbling began at the back of this strange thing, and the vessel began to move. We hung on for dear

72
00:06:12.508 --> 00:06:20.728
life. "As long as it sails horizontally, I don't mind," muttered Ned. "But if it decides to dive, we're in trouble."

73
00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:26.895
This long night continued to pass slowly. We clung to the strange vessel as it picked up speed.

74
00:06:26.896 --> 00:06:31.411
Several times I thought I heard some vague sounds coming from below us.

75
00:06:31.412 --> 00:06:36.482
It was almost as if someone had given a command and voices were responding.

76
00:06:36.483 --> 00:06:43.872
What kind of beings existed in this strange boat? I wondered. What was the source of its great speed?

77
00:06:43.873 --> 00:06:51.625
Daybreak finally appeared. I was about to examine the hull upon which we rested when I felt the whole vessel starting to sink.

78
00:06:51.626 --> 00:06:58.784
"Oh!" cried Ned, furiously kicking the metal beneath his feet. "Open up, you inhospitable rascals!"

79
00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:03.704
The sinking movement stopped at once. We heard the sound of metal moving.

80
00:07:03.705 --> 00:07:11.047
An iron plate slid aside and a man popped up. He uttered a strange cry and immediately disappeared.

81
00:07:11.048 --> 00:07:19.762
Moments later eight masked men appeared and silently forced the three of us into their strange machine.

